System Boot (Desktop and Mobile)
Version: 1.0
Purpose: Concise reference of how operating systems boot on PCs/servers and mobile devices.

PC/Server (Linux) Boot Flow
- Power On -> Firmware -> Bootloader -> Kernel -> initramfs -> Root FS -> PID 1 -> Services/Targets
- Firmware
    - BIOS (legacy, MBR) or UEFI (modern, GPT, NVRAM boot entries, Secure Boot)
    - POST hardware init, device discovery, boot order, hands off to bootloader
- Bootloaders
    - GRUB 2: menu, kernel and initrd loading, supports BIOS/UEFI
    - systemd-boot: simple UEFI boot manager (reads EFI system partition entries)
- Kernel and initramfs
    - Kernel decompresses, sets up drivers, memory, scheduler
    - initramfs provides early userspace: discovers root device, decrypts LUKS, assembles RAID/LVM
    - Switches root (pivot_root/switch_root) to real filesystem
- Userspace
    - PID 1: systemd (starts units/targets), or alternatives (runit, OpenRC, SysV)
    - Multi-user/graphical targets reached after services and display manager start

Key Linux Boot Artifacts
- Firmware: UEFI variables, BootOrder, Boot#### entries
- Partitions: ESP (EFI System Partition, FAT32), root (/), swap, /boot (optional)
- Files: /boot/vmlinuz-*, /boot/initrd.img-*, /etc/default/grub, /etc/fstab
- GRUB config generation: update-grub (grub-mkconfig), grub-install for (U)EFI/MBR
- Initramfs: update-initramfs (Ubuntu/Debian), dracut (Fedora/RHEL)
- Secure Boot: signed bootloader, kernel, and modules (shim + MOK on many distros)

Diagnostics (Linux)
- Firmware/boot: efibootmgr -v, fwupdmgr get-devices
- Storage: lsblk -f, blkid, cat /etc/fstab
- Kernel logs: dmesg, journalctl -b, journalctl -b -1 for previous boot
- Services: systemctl list-units --state=failed, systemd-analyze blame/critical-chain
- Initramfs content: lsinitramfs /boot/initrd.img-$(uname -r)
- GRUB: grep -E 'CMDLINE_LINUX|GRUB_' /etc/default/grub

Common Linux Boot Issues and Hints
- Wrong root= UUID/LABEL -> verify with blkid and /etc/fstab, regenerate grub/initramfs
- Missing drivers in initramfs (storage, filesystems, crypto) -> add hooks, update-initramfs -u -k all
- Encrypted/LVM/RAID not assembled -> ensure crypttab/lvm2/mdadm hooks included
- Secure Boot blocking unsigned modules -> sign modules or disable Secure Boot
- UEFI entry missing -> efibootmgr --create with correct --disk/--part and loader path (\EFI\...\grubx64.efi)

Android Boot Flow (Mobile)
- Boot ROM (immutable) -> OEM Bootloader (ABL/LK/U-Boot) -> Verified Boot (AVB) -> Kernel + initramfs -> init -> Zygote -> SystemServer -> Home
- Partitions (varies by device): boot, vendor_boot, dtbo, vbmeta, recovery, system, vendor, product
- Verified Boot: dm-verity and vbmeta enforce integrity
- Modes: normal, recovery, fastboot (flashing), download mode (OEM)
- Diagnostics: adb logcat, adb shell dmesg, getprop, last_kmsg (if available)

iOS Boot Flow (Mobile)
- Boot ROM (immutable, hardware root of trust) -> LLB/iBoot -> Secure Boot chain -> kernelcache -> launchd -> SpringBoard
- Strong code signing and integrity checks through the chain
- Modes: normal, Recovery, DFU (for restore)

General Concepts
- Chain of trust: each stage verifies next (UEFI Secure Boot, AVB, iOS secure boot)
- Device tree/ACPI: hardware description passed to kernel
- Kernel command line: controls root device, debug, SELinux, etc.
- Power states: cold boot vs warm boot; hibernate/suspend resume paths are distinct

Safe Recovery Checklist
1) Firmware: reset to defaults, confirm UEFI/Legacy mode matches install, check boot order
2) Storage: verify ESP presence and flags, partition UUIDs match fstab
3) Reinstall bootloader: for UEFI, mount ESP and run grub-install; then update-grub
4) Regenerate initramfs for current kernel
5) Review kernel cmdline for root=, resume=, cryptdevice= parameters
6) Check logs from last successful/failed boot with journalctl -b and -b -1

